[Music]  our other top story tonight discussions  about sending students  back to class it was the focus of  tonight's ccsd board of trustees meeting  in a matter of weeks the board could  vote to begin a slow transition  back in july the board decided it wanted  to meet certain requirements to return  one of those was to have a five percent  or less positivity rate  and a downward trend in cases for  fourteen days dr fuhrman lagun with the  southern nevada health district  says those requirements won't be met  anytime soon  but he did say he would support the  board's decision return  if they have a plan that includes  changing capacity and implementing  social distancing  board members made it clear there's no  easy answer  would it be more safe for everybody to  keep schools  closed or at least only  partially open um i don't know i just  feel really uncomfortable with this  whole thing  and it doesn't seem like there's a lot  of answers and i really don't like the  answer of  just reopen because it's not going to  get better this year  other trustees brought up the point that  many other public school districts  around the country remain closed even  with  much lower positivity rates than clark  county many don't plan to reopen for the  remainder of the year  as for csd ccsd a vote on a plan would  include some in-person instruction  should happen at the november 12th  meeting  there is no timeline for a return of the  classroom now it should be noted though  that  two new international medical studies  just released  show no consistent relationship between  in-person k-12 schooling  and the spread of covet and a third  study just released from the u.s shows  no elevated risk to child care workers  who stayed on the job additionally over  99 percent of schools around the country  that opened two months ago  remain open

